    "chunk_size_bytes": 1949,
    "chunk_text": "Gomez, a Fellow of the Sri Lanka Institute of Architects and principal architect of BGJF Consultancy Services, did not live to see the milestone. Dawoodbhoy asked guests to stand for a minute\u2019s silence in his memory, recalling a warm exchange with the architect the last time they spoke.\n\nThe Managing Director was candid that the venture was never built around returns.\n\n\u201cPassion was always the motive behind Aditya; profit was a secondary thing because all of us had other main avenues of income,\u201d he pointed out.\n\nHe paid tribute to the small group who poured \u201ctheir hearts and their passion\u201d into the project during construction, including interior designer Patricia Mamelka, who was in the audience.\n\nEach of the original suites takes its name from the Adityas, the solar deities born of the goddess Aditi in Hindu mythology, whom later tradition counts as twelve, matching the dozen suites with which the resort opened. Guests today sleep in Mitra, Varuna, Aryaman or Indra. Surya, the sun itself, crowns the Old Wing as a 1,800 square foot penthouse spread over two levels, with a private sundeck and an open-air plunge pool.\n\nBelow it, the Shanthi suites, of between 950 and 1,100 square feet, open onto private courtyards and plunge pools. The slightly larger Sagara suites occupy the mezzanine and first floor, their verandahs facing straight out to the Indian Ocean.\n\nThe interiors, by Patricia Mamelka, draw together Rajasthani antiques, Balinese contemporary pieces and the craft of the south. Ambalangoda masks are set against polished cement floors, alongside intricately carved bedheads and expansive open-plan spaces that let sea breezes and daylight move freely through the building. When the hotel was new, Mamelka described the idea as \u201ca mansion with big \u2018rooms\u2019 ... each having a character of its own\u201d, one that would offer foreign guests a window into Sri Lankan art and culture without leaving the property."
