    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "The interiors, by Patricia Mamelka, draw together Rajasthani antiques, Balinese contemporary pieces and the craft of the south. Ambalangoda masks are set against polished cement floors, alongside intricately carved bedheads and expansive open-plan spaces that let sea breezes and daylight move freely through the building. When the hotel was new, Mamelka described the idea as \u201ca mansion with big \u2018rooms\u2019 ... each having a character of its own\u201d, one that would offer foreign guests a window into Sri Lankan art and culture without leaving the property.\n\nAditya also dispensed with fixed meal times from the outset. In its first year a co-manager remarked that breakfast at 6 p.m. was entirely possible, with tables laid wherever a guest chose, whether on the beach, in the garden or in the suite.\n\nOutside the property, the resort arranges whale watching off Mirissa, boat safaris on the Madu Ganga, village cycling tours and guided visits to the Galle Fort. A spa and a preventative health and wellness programme cater to guests who would rather not leave the grounds at all.\n\nWhen Aditya opened, boutique hospitality in Sri Lanka was still in its infancy, with properties of the kind only beginning to appear around the turn of the century. Dawoodbhoy pitched the resort as an answer to a shortage of private getaways for upmarket travellers seeking seclusion.\n\nIts formula was simple: very few keys, unusually generous suites with private pools, design-led interiors and unscripted personal service on a secluded beach. That formula has since become a familiar template down south, where boutique hotels and private villas now court the same affluent traveller along much of the coast between Galle and Tangalle. UK specialist operator Experience Travel Group, for one, credits Aditya with raising the bar on the island\u2019s early boutique concept."
