    "md5": "a2bbcabfcf7f3fc657760e0f3c37d7f4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "The monthly increase was driven overwhelmingly by the non-food items, which added 0.33 percentage points to the overall movement. Food contributed just 0.02 percentage points, as the declines in several staples and protein sources largely offset the increases in the fresh produce.\n\nRestaurants and hotels made the largest contribution to the monthly rise, at 0.17 percentage points, followed by transport, at 0.13 percentage points.\n\nSmaller upward contributions came from health, household furnishings and maintenance, recreation and culture, miscellaneous goods and services and alcoholic beverages and tobacco.\n\nThe housing and utilities category provided some relief during the month, making a negative contribution of 0.01 percentage points, with the lower LP gas prices among the factors reducing expenditure. The education prices remained unchanged, while clothing, footwear and communication recorded slight increases.\n\nLimes contributed 0.14 percentage points to the monthly increase in the overall index, followed by vegetables at 0.11 percentage points and fresh fruits at 0.06 percentage points. Bread, coconuts and red onions also exerted the upward pressure.\n\nHowever, the falling sea fish prices reduced the index by 0.25 percentage points. The lower prices for green chillies and eggs each subtracted 0.06 percentage points, while chicken contributed a further decline of 0.03 percentage points. The prices of prawns, potatoes and coconut oil also fell.\n\nThose offsets kept the net monthly food contribution small, even as the food prices remained substantially higher than a year earlier.\n\nOn an annual basis, food contributed 2.87 percentage points to headline inflation. Sea fish was the largest individual upward contributor within the food basket, adding 0.74 percentage points, followed by vegetables at 0.52 percentage points, fresh fruits at 0.43 percentage points and dried fish at 0.37 percentage points."
