    "md5": "bb5f70dbb5781e660a7e096acdd8563c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "While the Port Authority plans for a PPP in place of the abandoned joint venture for a logistical hub, the authorities should make sure the project would not be a repetition of the recurrent institutional maladies.\n\nThe general obsession to read geopolitics into this recurrent failure and procrastination of high-profile foreign projects is absurd is because they have affected projects with every major country \u2014 China, India, Japan and USA \u2014 that had sought to meaningfully engage economically with Sri Lanka. Similarly, it conceals the tremendous economic cost of the institutional incompetence and arcane regulatory framework. Until you recognise the principal malady, it is not possible to fix it.\n\nAnother major economic opportunity being pursued half-heartedly at the moment is the Japanese proposal for a Japan, Sri Lanka and India export- oriented economic corridor intended to integrate Sri Lanka with high- value Japanese supply chains and industrial hubs in India. The project, announced during President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s visit to Japan and backed by Japan\u2019s Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, aims to position Sri Lanka as a strategic manufacturing hub, and has the potential to increase Sri Lanka\u2019s GDP by 9.3 per cent by 2030.\n\nHowever, the project, like many of its predecessors, has faced the classic Sri Lankan \u2018policy to implementation trap\u2019.\u00a0 A similar project in the past for strategic development in Trincomalee involving India, Japan and Sri Lanka had been a talk shop during much of Ranil Wickremesinghe\u2019s administration and is now being rehashed into an energy hub involving Dubai, India and Sri Lanka, though on both occasions, there is little tangible progress on the ground.\n\nJapan has insisted on legally binding guarantees for transparency, predictability and non-discrimination. The recent Investment Protection Act and the proposed Public Private Partnership Bill are intended to address some of the deep-rooted bureaucratic bottlenecks."
  },
