    "chunk_size_bytes": 1687,
    "chunk_text": "Ranasingha says the stress is impacting her health. \"I am scared something is going to happen to me,\" she says.\n\n\"I don't know what her solution is, to be honest,\" says Auckland-based immigration law specialist Alastair McClymont.\n\nThe BBC reached out to Gallant but did not receive a response.\n\nMcClymont suggests New Zealand authorities will not grant the transit visa because, as far as they are concerned, she has no intention of returning to Sri Lanka having made an asylum claim saying her life was in danger there.\n\nNew Zealand co-ordinates with its neighbour Australia which means it won't want to let her transit either, he says, and France and the US are likely to reach similar conclusions.\n\nTo leave, \"she is going to have to transit through somewhere and no-one will want to take the risk.\"\n\nImmigration New Zealand told the BBC it had received multiple transit visa applications from Ranasingha - all declined \"as we were not satisfied she has a genuine intention to transit through New Zealand\".\n\nWhen asked why one couldn't be granted even when Ranasingha had offered to be handcuffed and escorted, a representative of the immigration minister's office said those measures do not prevent individuals from making an asylum claim.\n\nAustralia's interior ministry said it would not comment on individual cases. It said any non-citizens travelling to Australia \"must satisfy the requirements of the Migration Act 1958 (the Act) and the Migration Regulations 1994\".\n\nMeanwhile, Ranasingha's family have been trying to see if the Sri Lankan government can help. Her brother says they have tried \"every possible avenue... but so far we haven't been able to get anywhere on this\"."
