    "chunk_size_bytes": 1862,
    "chunk_text": "Officials said the windows of a gun shop were smashed and that they had seized two weapons after arresting a suspect. People also broke into other shops, stealing trainers and other items, local media reported.\n\nPoseck said: \"We all have those images from France in our minds - looting, burning houses, burning police vehicles, and attacks on police officers... France must not be held up as a model in this regard.\"\n\nPolice said the situation calmed down overnight into Sunday in the city.\n\nSimilar instances took place in other German cities, following earlier largely peaceful demonstrations.\n\nIn Cologne, police said officers were attacked near the main train station, with bottles thrown and a bicycle launched.\n\nCologne's police chief Johannes Hermanns said it was \"deeply saddening that young people who wanted to peacefully demonstrate for their future were infiltrated today by extremists who had nothing but violence in mind\".\n\nA smaller demonstration in the evening in Berlin - following larger protests in the day - resulted in 27 police officers being injured, and up to 56 people temporarily detained, Berlin police said.\n\nIn Hamburg, people dressed in black set off fireworks and set barricades on fire, with public broadcaster NDR reporting that 120 people were arrested.\n\nThe protests in Germany took place on the same day as other large-scale demonstrations in Europe.\n\nIn France, more than 320,000 people took part in a third consecutive weekend of largely peaceful protests in cities across the country, demanding action on the high cost of living and poor public services, especially in the education sector.\n\nThousands of police officers were deployed across France to deal with the demonstrations, and officers fired tear gas at demonstrators in some cities.\n\nThere were more than 500 arrests after clashes in Toulouse, Lyon and Marseille."
