    "md5": "346de4b5c7b390081b889337386b851f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2009,
    "chunk_text": "One dead, 18 injured during stampede in Sialkot at TikTok star\u2019s wedding event\n\nARAB NEWS - One person was killed while 18 were injured this week in a stampede during a wedding event of a TikTok star in Pakistan\u2019s eastern city of Sialkot, police said on Sunday, booking organizers for violating safety standard operating procedures.\n\nThe incident took place on Saturday evening as a large crowd assembled for a wedding event of Ansar Mehmood, who uses the alias of \u201cMr. Pattlo\u201d on TikTok, at the Faraz Cricket Ground on Daska Road.\n\nPolice said in a statement on Saturday that as per preliminary investigations, event organizers invited a large number of people by advertising prize draws for motorcycles, cars and other valuable items at the event through social media. During the event, police said an announcement made over the loudspeaker asked participants to enter the ground simultaneously, triggering a stampede.\n\nOne person was killed while 18 others were injured in the stampede, the police statement said.\n\n\u201cSix people have been arrested in connection with the incident, while TikToker Ansar Mehmood alias Pattlo, who allegedly invited people to the wedding celebration through social media, remains at large,\u201d Shahid Mehmood, a local police officer, told Arab News.\n\nThe injured individuals were provided medical assistance and have been discharged from hospitals, police said. The stampede disrupted traffic on the main Daska-Sialkot road, while incidents of disorder and damage to public property were also reported, it added.\n\nCases have been registered against nine individuals, including Mehmood, the ground owner, other organizers and 40 to 45 unidentified security guards, police said. Suspects have been charged under sections 322, 431, 440, 188, 109, 148 and 149 of the Pakistan Penal Code, as well as Section 16 of the Maintenance of Public Order.\n\n\u201cThe charges include violations of safety standard operating procedures (SOPs) and permit conditions,\u201d the statement said."
