    "md5": "8aaf2f83721cf88316a67c6e5e4d259b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "However, Mageean's early progress in the senior ranks was hampered by a serious ankle injury, which ended her hopes of making her Olympic debut at London 2012.\n\nHaving had surgery in 2013, Mageean won the Irish 1500m title on her return in July 2014.\n\nIn late 2017, Mageean left her Dublin base to join Steve Vernon's Team New Balance in Manchester after parting ways with coach Jerry Kiernan.\n\nAnd while she endured some disappointing results following the move, Mageean claimed a European Indoor bronze medal in March 2019 before clocking two minutes 31.06 seconds to take 3.6 seconds off Sonia O'Sullivan's 27-year-old Irish 1,000m record at the 2020 Diamond League Monaco meet.\n\nMageean endured more Olympic heartache at the delayed Tokyo Games in 2021, when a calf injury disrupted her preparations as she exited from the heats.\n\nHowever, she enjoyed a successful 2022 campaign under the guidance of former Great Britain Olympian Helen Clitheroe, winning 1500m European and Commonwealth silver behind Laura Muir.\n\nTwo weeks after the Euros, Mageean beat Muir and a world-class field to seal a stunning 1500m victory at Diamond League Brussels. Her time of 3:56.63 took 2.22 seconds off O'Sullivan's Irish record and marked her first sub four-minute run.\n\nIn 2023, Mageean narrowly missed out on a World Championship medal as she finished fourth in a high-class final in Budapest.\n\nHowever, after taking over two seconds off O'Sullivan's Irish mile record in July, Mageean was named the 2023 BBC Northern Ireland Sports Personality of the Year.\n\nAfter a string of near-misses at championship level, Mageean secured the biggest win of her career at the 2024 European Championships in Rome.\n\nIn a gripping final, Mageean found herself boxed in behind Great Britain duo Georgia Bell and Jemma Reekie, but managed to dart through a gap and power down the straight to victory in 4:04.66."
  },
