    "chunk_text": "This problem is not new. In ancient Lanka, courtiers often assured kings that their subjects were content while concealing their hardships. Wise rulers therefore travelled through villages at night in disguise, discovering conditions for themselves and addressing grievances before they threatened the realm.\n\nMacroeconomic data alone cannot reveal conditions at ground level. A close examination of cases involving frustrated citizens shows discontent that official statistics rarely capture but that dominates the daily lives of many families.\n\nThree broad causes recur: corrupt private-sector practices that cheat consumers and workers; public-sector failures that subject citizens to needless hardship; and banking rigidities that drive vulnerable borrowers into the hands of loan sharks.\n\nBecause these grievances are seldom assembled into formal macroeconomic assessments, they often fail to reach policymakers. The result is a dangerous disconnect between reassuring economic indicators at the top and painful realities at the bottom.\n\nThe private sector, widely regarded as the engine of growth in Sri Lanka, contributes more than three-quarters of the country\u2019s national wealth. Yet,\n\nunresolved governance failures in some businesses leave workers and consumers vulnerable. Two examples illustrate the problem: the exploitation of young workers by small garment factories operating outside Colombo, and the defrauding of customers by corrupt insurance agents."
