    "chunk_text": "The exploitation thrives in the absence of strict, modern usury laws and effective enforcement. Even the historic Ultra Duplum rule\u2014which limits accumulated interest to the original principal\u2014is bypassed or poorly enforced under the century-old Money Lending Ordinance and newer microfinance regulation to be enforced under the recently enacted Microfinance Regulatory Authority Act. Vulnerable borrowers rarely possess the knowledge, records, time, or resources needed to challenge abusive terms. The resulting anger is widespread in rural and urban communities. As the economics writer Yadamini Gunawardena observed by coining the term \u201cThe 5km Blind Spot\u201d, such exploitation occurs within five kilometres of Colombo\u2019s skyscraper-filled financial district\u2014a systemic failure under policymakers\u2019 noses. Financial inclusion cannot be measured only by the number of bank branches, accounts, or digital applications; it must also be judged by whether citizens can obtain safe, affordable credit when they need it.\n\nTaken together, these ground-level realities reveal a fractured recovery. The bird\u2019s-eye view of lenders and policymakers shows fiscal discipline, single-digit inflation, rising reserves, and renewed growth. The worm\u2019s-eye view shows ordinary citizens squeezed by private corruption, public-sector failure, arbitrary regulation, and financial exclusion. Macroeconomic stability is essential, but it is not sufficient if people continue to experience insecurity, exploitation, and ineffective public services. This \u2018not-so-rosy picture\u2019 is the breeding ground for social, economic, and political disorders that will disable overnight, as happened in 2022, the much-praised stable and growing economy.\n\n(The writer, a former Deputy Governor of the Central bank of Sri Lanka, can be reached at [email\u00a0protected] )"
