    "md5": "16a24ad20487ff2794f4904690103be9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1355,
    "chunk_text": "The organised crime bill also contains sections that could curtail freedom of expression by making it an offense to publish the name or anything that could identify a victim of crime. Further restrictions are placed on media reporting of court proceedings without court permission.\n\nRepealing the PTA would fulfill a key commitment that Sri Lanka made to the European Union, in exchange for tariff-free access to the EU market under its Generalised Scheme of Preferences Plus (GSP+) program. However, the Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill preserves the terrorism act\u2019s abusive features, further weakening Sri Lanka\u2019s compliance with its GSP+ human rights commitments.\n\nThe Government did not appear to conduct consultations, while drafting the bill. The Government should withdraw the bill, undertake a genuine, open, and inclusive consultation, ensure legislation with robust safeguards to prevent arbitrary detention, torture, and ill treatment, and ensure judicial oversight and due process, in compliance with the Government\u2019s international legal obligations.\n\n\u201cEvery Government has a duty to combat organised crime, but needs to do so in compliance with international human rights standards,\u201d Pearson said. \u201cThe European Union should make it clear that this bill would not meet Sri Lanka\u2019s obligations under the GSP+ trade program.\u201d"
  },
