    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "COYLE\u2019s \u201cI AM\u201d Leadership Forum 2026 concludes with a focus on stories that shape leaders\n\nChief Guest Indian High Commissioner of India to Sri Lanka Santosh Jha (centre) along with COYLE Chairman Suren Chandraratna (fourth from right) , COYLE Committee Chairman Raveen Wickramasinghe (left), Sensa Solutions Managing Director Subash Thalangama (third from right), Tudawe Brothers CEO Romali Tudawe (second fro right), and hSenid Group of Companies Founder/Chairman Dinesh Saparamadu (second from left)\n\nThe Chamber of Lankan Entrepreneurs (COYLE) concluded its \u201cI AM\u201d Leadership Forum 2026 at Cinnamon Grand Colombo last week, bringing together entrepreneurs and business leaders for an evening of personal stories, shared experience and dialogue on leadership. The event was made possible by principle sponsor, Nations Trust Bank, along with print media sponsors, DailyFT and The Sunday Times.\n\nHeld under the theme \u201cMy Story. My Journey\u201d, the forum reflected COYLE\u2019s broader vision for 2026 of \u201cBridging Generations. Building Futures\u201d, creating space for established and emerging entrepreneurs to learn from one another.\n\nIndian High Commissioner Santosh Jha, graced the occasion as Chief Guest and keynote speaker. His participation brought an international dimension to the forum and underscored the value of dialogue between the diplomatic and business communities.\n\nIn his welcome address, COYLE Chairman Suren Chandraratna challenged participants to consider the question at the heart of the forum: \u201cWho am I?\u201d Drawing on his own experience as a panellist the previous year, he described the \u2018I AM\u2019 platform as an opportunity to reflect on what entrepreneurs have achieved, overcome and become. \u201cWhen a person discovers their \u2018I AM\u2019, they stop being defined by where they come from, what others said they could be, or limitations placed upon them. They begin to see not just who they are today, but who they can become tomorrow,\u201d he said."
  },
