    "chunk_size_bytes": 1780,
    "chunk_text": "Proposed Organised Crimes Bill faces mounting challenges before Supreme Court\n\nColombo, Nov. 2 (Daily Mirror) - Special Determination petitions are mounting before the Supreme Court challenging the constitutionality of the proposed Prevention of Organised Crimes Bill, with petitioners raising concerns that several provisions could undermine fundamental rights, judicial oversight and media freedom.\n\nAmong these petitions, two journalists, Tharindu Uduwaragedara and Mohamed Fazeer, have filed another Special Determination petition seeking a declaration that several clauses of the proposed Bill are inconsistent with the provisions of the Constitution.\n\nFiling this petition through Gowry Shankari Law Associates, the petitioners contend that Clause 56 of the proposed Bill, which criminalises the disclosure of information likely to prejudice an investigation conducted or proposed to be conducted under the Act, could have serious implications for journalists reporting on matters of public interest.\n\nThey argue that although the provision contains certain protections for legal communications and a defence based on the absence of knowledge or reasonable grounds for suspicion, it does not expressly provide protection for public-interest reporting.\n\nAccordingly, they contend that the broad scope of the provision could create uncertainty for journalists, family members and others seeking to disclose information concerning an investigation, and that it is inconsistent with Articles 12(1) and 14(1)(a) of the Constitution.\n\nThe petitioners have also challenged several other provisions of the Bill, arguing that they confer excessive powers on law enforcement authorities and executive officials while restricting judicial supervision over investigations and detention."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-8d6b556f",
