    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b9a3b22ae1881100bd8738b680f41309",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "The petitioners have also challenged several other provisions of the Bill, arguing that they confer excessive powers on law enforcement authorities and executive officials while restricting judicial supervision over investigations and detention.\n\nBill allows organised crime links to be inferred from dress, tattoos and associations\n\nThey further challenge Clause 4(2), which permits membership of an organised criminal group to be inferred from factors such as association with known members, dress, hand signs, language, tattoos, visits to certain premises and repeated arrests while in the company of such members.\n\nThe petitioners argue that these factors do not, by themselves, establish membership of an organised criminal group and could lead to arbitrary treatment.\n\nThey have also questioned Clause 17, which empowers police officers, on reasonable suspicion of a serious crime, to stop and search, question and arrest individuals, search premises without a warrant and seize relevant items.\n\nWith regard to detention, the petitioners challenge Clause 20, which permits a suspect to be held for up to 72 hours before being produced before a Magistrate, excluding journey time, with a further 24 hours of police custody if authorised.\n\nThey argue that the provision unduly restricts the Magistrate's judicial discretion by limiting the available options and preventing an independent determination of whether a suspect should instead be released on bail or discharged.\n\nThe petitioners have also challenged Clause 21, which empowers the Secretary to the Ministry responsible for Police to order the detention of a suspect for periods of up to two months at a time, for a maximum period of one year, to facilitate investigations, obtain evidence or question the suspect.\n\nThey contend that the provision allows an executive official, rather than a court, to determine whether a person should remain in detention for investigative purposes."
  },
