    "chunk_text": "The petitioners have also challenged Clause 21, which empowers the Secretary to the Ministry responsible for Police to order the detention of a suspect for periods of up to two months at a time, for a maximum period of one year, to facilitate investigations, obtain evidence or question the suspect.\n\nThey contend that the provision allows an executive official, rather than a court, to determine whether a person should remain in detention for investigative purposes.\n\nPolice empowers to obtain information from service providers without court order\n\nConcerns have also been raised over Clause 44(4), which empowers a Deputy Inspector General of Police or a higher-ranking officer to obtain subscriber identity details, call records and tower-location information from service providers without obtaining prior authorisation from a Magistrate.\n\nThe petitioners argue that this could result in the surveillance of individuals who have no connection whatsoever with organised criminal activity, without adequate safeguards to protect their privacy.\n\nThey have also challenged provisions relating to access to encrypted services, interception of communications, the admissibility of statements and other forms of evidence, arguing that these could undermine procedural safeguards and the rights of accused persons.\n\nThe petitioners are seeking a determination that the proposed Bill, as presently constituted, or one or more of its provisions, is inconsistent with the Constitution and that it could be enacted only with the special majority required under Article 84(2) of the Constitution and approval by the People at a referendum to become law.\n\nThe petitioners are represented by Counsel Hejaaz Hizbullah with Shifan Mahroof, Shalini Roshana Fernando, Nehansa Weerasinghe, Uvindu Kuruwitabandara, Marshadha Mackie, Oshadhi Ganisha Hapuarachchi and Alagaratnam Jinoshan instructed by Tharmarajah Tharmaja."
