    "md5": "e1db895d6a512fbf1e7ccb5b999435d9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1489,
    "chunk_text": "WHEN A CRISIS WON\u2019T GO AWAY\n\nMost organisations facing a serious crisis would welcome a few days away from the cameras. Manchester City has no such luxury. Its manager still has to face journalists before matches, its players must perform in front of thousands of supporters, and its employees have to answer questions from friends and family about what is happening at their club. All this is taking place while Manchester City appeals an independent commission\u2019s findings that it seriously breached the Premier League\u2019s financial rules. The club maintains its innocence, but the reputational consequences are already being felt. This week on our BBC 4 radio show, When It Hits the Fan, my co-host David Yelland and I discussed what happens when a crisis becomes part of an organisation\u2019s everyday life. We also looked at how Marks & Spencer is turning its employees into social media stars, and why the government\u2019s advice to prepare for emergencies is finding a more receptive audience than it might have a few years ago.\n\nThe commission's findings have generated damaging headlines for City, while the club's response has been uncompromising. Both sides have committed themselves publicly to their positions, leaving little room for compromise.\u00a0 I've been in meetings where executives are so convinced of their position that even discussing alternatives feels disloyal. Advisers can become caught up in that confidence too, making it harder to raise uncomfortable questions."
  },
