    "md5": "1f22b393834b8466f11153d2b4d86da4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1921,
    "chunk_text": "I have also sat in rooms with clients who listen intently when their lawyers speak but show considerably less interest when communications advisers raise concerns. Legal proceedings have established rules and outcomes. Public opinion is less predictable, and a favourable judgement does not necessarily restore confidence. Manchester City may succeed in its appeal, or the commission's findings may stand. The Premier League also has its credibility as a regulator at stake. Whatever the outcome, both organisations will have to contend with how the dispute has shaped public perceptions of them.\n\nThere are risks, of course. Most companies have rules about who can speak on their behalf, and an employee with a substantial personal following presents new questions about editorial freedom and corporate oversight. Too much control can make the content feel manufactured, while too little can leave both the employee and employer exposed if controversy arises. What M&S has done particularly well is allow Turner's personality and expertise to come through. She can speak about the products with an authority that would be difficult to replicate through a conventional celebrity endorsement.\n\nThe British government has been encouraging households to prepare for emergencies, advising people to keep essentials such as drinking water, non-perishable food, torches and wind-up radios at home.\n\nNot long ago, such advice might have sounded rather dramatic. But after Covid, when supermarket shelves emptied and everyday essentials suddenly became difficult to find, I suspect more people are inclined to take it seriously. The government's Be Ready campaign encourages households to check what they already have and gradually build up supplies, rather than rush out and stockpile. The language is deliberately measured, recognising that warnings about emergencies can easily create the anxiety they are intended to prevent."
