    "md5": "17e565a5c335e91f3ffe1477c639d089",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1898,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has world\u2019s most expensive cigarettes\n\nColombo, Nov. 2 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka has the most expensive cigarettes in the world when measured against purchasing power parity (PPP), according to the latest World Health Organisation (WHO) data cited in a new Oxford Economics assessment that questions the effectiveness of the WHO\u2019s global tobacco tax incidence benchmark.\n\nThe assessment argues that national economic realities, rather than a uniform global target, should guide tobacco taxation policies, highlighting significant differences in economic conditions across countries.\n\nIt questions the effectiveness of the WHO\u2019s 75% tobacco tax incidence benchmark, stating that achieving a specific tax incidence rate does not necessarily translate into lower smoking prevalence.\n\nAccording to the research, there is little meaningful relationship between the proportion of taxes in cigarette retail prices and overall smoking rates. Countries with higher tax incidence rates do not necessarily record lower levels of smoking, the report noted.\n\nThe findings raise questions about whether tax incidence alone should be used as an indicator of success in tobacco control and whether a single global benchmark can effectively guide taxation strategies across vastly different economies.\n\nThe assessment also highlights concerns previously raised by several international organisations regarding reliance on tax incidence targets to measure tobacco control effectiveness.\n\nAmong the publications cited is a WHO document which concluded that the share of taxes in the retail price of cigarettes is not, in itself, an important indicator of tobacco control success.\n\nFurthermore, the publication emphasised that the retail price of cigarettes, rather than the proportion of that price attributable to taxes, plays a more significant role in influencing smokers\u2019 consumption decisions."
