    "chunk_text": "Furthermore, the publication emphasised that the retail price of cigarettes, rather than the proportion of that price attributable to taxes, plays a more significant role in influencing smokers\u2019 consumption decisions.\n\nThe WHO\u2019s own Technical Manual on tobacco taxation also acknowledges that the tax share of retail prices is insufficient, on its own, to guarantee the success of policies aimed at reducing tobacco demand.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s position in global cigarette pricing, as highlighted in the assessment, underscores the importance of considering national economic conditions and consumer purchasing power when evaluating tobacco taxation policies.\n\nThe Oxford Economics assessment questions whether the WHO\u2019s 75% tax incidence benchmark can serve as a meaningful universal standard for countries with substantially different economic circumstances.\n\nMoreover, it argues that effective tobacco taxation strategies should be evaluated in the context of national realities, including cigarette prices, consumer purchasing power and smoking prevalence.\n\nWhile taxation remains an important instrument in tobacco control, the assessment indicates that a country\u2019s progress should not be judged solely by the percentage of cigarette retail prices accounted for by taxes.\n\nInstead, the relationship between taxation, retail prices and smoking behaviour should remain central to evaluating the effectiveness of tobacco control policies."
