    "md5": "4afc71984a7c05c0822b423058a1f536",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1244,
    "chunk_text": "ComBank launches dedicated loan scheme to revitalise tea industry\n\nCommercial Bank Deputy General Manager Personal Banking S. Ganeshan at the signing of a loan agreement with Jayasinghe Tea Factory, in the presence of (from left) Sri Lanka Tea Factory Owners Association Coordinating Manager Harshi Perera, Jayasinghe Tea Factory Partner Amal Jayasinghe, Sri Lanka Tea Factory Owners Association Chairman Lionel Herath, Commercial Bank Assistant General Manager Personal Banking Mithila Shamini, Senior Manager Malika De Silva and Development Credit Department Manager W.T.C. Lasantha\n\nIndustry-linked financing initiative targets renewal of ageing tea fields, improve productivity and empower tea smallholders\n\nCommercial Bank of Ceylon has introduced a dedicated loan scheme to revitalise Sri Lanka\u2019s tea industry, demonstrating its strategic initiatives designed to penetrate the small and medium enterprise sector and rural micro-economy and catalyse sustainable growth across the national tea industry.\n\nThe new loan scheme enables the tea smallholders to invest in the renewal of ageing tea fields, improve cultivation practices and strengthen the productivity and sustainability of one of the country\u2019s most important export sectors."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-d4441739",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d4441739",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "ComBank launches dedicated loan scheme to revitalise tea industry",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/ComBank-launches-dedicated-loan-scheme-to-revitalise-tea-industry/215-353948",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793602398.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-d4441739-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c5d3f02c8c02dd4b6c005fb7975998d3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "The new loan scheme enables the tea smallholders to invest in the renewal of ageing tea fields, improve cultivation practices and strengthen the productivity and sustainability of one of the country\u2019s most important export sectors.\n\nThe tea smallholders account for more than 70 percent of national tea production, making their capacity to maintain and improve their lands central to the future of the industry. By directing financing towards essential cultivation improvements, Commercial Bank said this timely initiative aims to help address these challenges while safeguarding the rural communities whose livelihoods depend on tea.\n\nThe scheme\u2019s distinguishing feature is its industry-linked financing model, which brings together the bank\u2019s financial expertise, SLTFOA\u2019s industry network and tea factories maintained by the tea smallholders.\n\nMoving beyond the standard banking services, this collaborative approach is designed to connect the eligible farmers with the funding they need to renew their tea lands and improve productivity.\n\nThe financing is available for replanting and infilling, soil rehabilitation, land preparation, purchase of certified planting material and agricultural inputs, nursery establishment, mechanisation, modern agricultural practices and irrigation systems. These investments are intended to help the farmers replace the ageing bushes, fill vacant spaces in the existing fields and make more productive use of their tea-growing land.\n\nUnder the scheme, the eligible tea smallholders can obtain loans of up to Rs.1 million per acre for replanting and infilling or up to Rs.1.5 million per acre for replanting and infilling with an irrigation system.\n\nThe loan scheme has been designed with tailor-made features to support tea cultivation projects. The repayments will be made through deductions linked to green-leaf deliveries to factories, with a corporate guarantee from the tea factory serving as security."
