    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Construction growth slows in September amid bitumen shortage\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s construction activity expanded at a slower pace for the second consecutive month in September, with the Construction Purchasing Managers\u2019 Index (PMI) easing to 57.8 from 59.4 in August, while a persistent shortage of bitumen delayed several road projects, Central Bank of Sri Lanka (CBSL) data showed.\n\nThe September reading marked the slowest pace of expansion since March, when the index stood at 57.1, and was 3.6 points below July\u2019s 61.4. When compared to the same month last year, the index was 9.8 points lower than the 67.6 recorded in September 2025, which at the time marked the strongest rise in activity since late 2021. A reading above 50 denotes expansion on a month-on-month basis.\n\nAccording to CBSL, growth was supported by a sustained flow of new construction projects. However, the shortage of bitumen remained a major constraint, a bottleneck that survey respondents have repeatedly flagged since mid-year.\n\nIn contrast, the New Orders Index rose to 65.6 from 62.5 in August, driven primarily by government-funded projects, marking its third successive monthly gain. The gap between new orders and overall activity widened to 7.8 points from 3.1 points a month earlier, suggesting that fresh work was coming in faster than contractors were able to execute it. Hiring also moderated, with the Employment Index easing to 58.1 from 59.7 in August and 61.8 in July. Recruitment was supported by steady project availability, although many firms continued to cite shortages of skilled workers, the Central Bank noted.\n\nThe Quantity of Purchases Index increased to 62.5 from 59.4 in August, reflecting procurement requirements for ongoing and newly awarded projects.\n\nMeanwhile, supply-side pressures intensified, with the Suppliers\u2019 Delivery Time Index climbing 6.2 points to 65.6, its highest reading since May\u2019s 71.2, signalling that delivery times lengthened at a faster rate than in August."
