    "chunk_text": "Current account slips back into US$ 128mn deficit in September\n\nDeficit was the smallest of the five monthly shortfalls recorded this year, with April\u2019s revised US$ 572mn the largest\n\nNine-month trade deficit widened 53% to US$ 7.9bn as import expenditure rose 21.3%, outpacing 5.3% growth in exports\n\nFuel imports cost approximately US$ 4.5bn in the first nine months, up 51.5% YoY, while vehicle imports reached US$ 1.87bn\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s external current account slipped back into a deficit of US$ 128 million in September, after a brief US$ 133 million surplus in August, taking the cumulative shortfall for the first nine months to US$ 420 million against a US$ 1.85 billion surplus a year earlier, Central Bank data showed.\n\nThe September deficit was the fifth recorded this year but the smallest of the five, well below the revised US$ 572 million shortfall in April, the first of the year. It was also narrower than the US$ 194 million deficit in September 2025, as a lower trade deficit and higher remittances outweighed a wider primary income deficit and a smaller services surplus.\n\nMerchandise export earnings rose 21.0 percent year-on-year to US$ 1.38 billion during the month, the highest monthly figure on record and around US$ 75 million above the previous peak set in July 2025. The Export Development Board had earlier estimated September merchandise exports at US$ 1.39 billion. Import expenditure, meanwhile, rose a more modest 2.9 percent to US$ 2.11 billion. With exports far outpacing imports, the monthly trade deficit narrowed 19.6 percent to US$ 731 million from US$ 910 million a year earlier. However, the cumulative trade deficit widened 52.9 percent to US$ 7.91 billion during January-September from US$ 5.17 billion in the corresponding period of 2025. Import expenditure rose 21.3 percent to US$ 18.67 billion over the nine months, an increase of US$ 3.28 billion, while export earnings grew only 5.3 percent to US$ 10.75 billion."
