    "chunk_text": "Merchandise export earnings rose 21.0 percent year-on-year to US$ 1.38 billion during the month, the highest monthly figure on record and around US$ 75 million above the previous peak set in July 2025. The Export Development Board had earlier estimated September merchandise exports at US$ 1.39 billion. Import expenditure, meanwhile, rose a more modest 2.9 percent to US$ 2.11 billion. With exports far outpacing imports, the monthly trade deficit narrowed 19.6 percent to US$ 731 million from US$ 910 million a year earlier. However, the cumulative trade deficit widened 52.9 percent to US$ 7.91 billion during January-September from US$ 5.17 billion in the corresponding period of 2025. Import expenditure rose 21.3 percent to US$ 18.67 billion over the nine months, an increase of US$ 3.28 billion, while export earnings grew only 5.3 percent to US$ 10.75 billion.\n\nFuel now makes up close to a quarter of the nine-month import bill, above the average share of around 20 percent it has held annually over the past decade, as per Central Bank data.\n\nMonthly fuel spending has nonetheless eased, with expenditure declining for the fifth consecutive month in September. Fuel imports had peaked at US$ 886 million in April amid higher oil prices and import volumes following the outbreak of the Middle East conflict in late February, before moderating to US$ 536 million in May, US$ 465 million in June and US$ 453 million in July.\n\nExpenditure on personal and commercial vehicle imports fell 34.4 percent year-on-year to US$ 188 million in September from US$ 286 million a year earlier. The September figure was broadly in line with the US$ 189 million spent in August and well below the US$ 241 million recorded in July."
