    "chunk_text": "Expenditure on personal and commercial vehicle imports fell 34.4 percent year-on-year to US$ 188 million in September from US$ 286 million a year earlier. The September figure was broadly in line with the US$ 189 million spent in August and well below the US$ 241 million recorded in July.\n\nCentral Bank Governor Dr. Nandalal Weerasinghe has attributed the slowdown in vehicle imports to tighter loan-to-value rules and a government surcharge, noting that fewer letters of credit had been opened in recent months, which pointed to lower imports ahead. In May, he had warned that vehicle imports could reach about US$ 2.4 billion this year if the pace seen in the early months continued.\n\nThe terms of trade deteriorated both in September and over the nine months, as import prices rose faster than export prices, the Central Bank said.\n\nThe primary income deficit widened to US$ 241 million from US$ 152 million a year earlier, mainly due to higher dividend payments on direct investment, with direct investment income outflows more than doubling to US$ 191.5 million.\n\nWorkers\u2019 remittances rose 5.6 percent year-on-year to US$ 734 million in September, slowing from 10.0 percent growth in August, but they were still sufficient on their own to offset the month\u2019s trade deficit. Cumulative remittances increased 18.1 percent to US$ 6.87 billion during the nine months, although the Central Bank noted that the figure may include other inflows, such as those received following Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nIn contrast, the services account surplus fell 34.3 percent to US$ 119 million, as outflows rose 36.7 percent to US$ 441 million against an 11.2 percent increase in inflows to US$ 559 million. Overseas travel expenditure jumped 64.9 percent to US$ 122 million, equivalent to about two-thirds of what the country earned from tourism during the month. The cumulative services surplus declined 22.3 percent to US$ 2.21 billion."
