    "md5": "2dc106bc2736a4af77b1cdd4350f6c3e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1875,
    "chunk_text": "Pan Asia Bank 3Q profit rises 25%\n\nPan Asia Banking Corporation PLC\u2019s September-quarter net profit rose 25 percent from a year earlier, supported by an impairment reversal, while rising funding costs and operating expenses weighed on income growth.\n\nThe bank reported earnings of Rs. 1.09 billion for the three months ended September 30, 2026, against Rs. 872.5 million a year earlier. Earnings per share increased to Rs. 2.47 from Rs. 1.97.\n\nAn impairment reversal of Rs. 367.2 million, compared with a charge of Rs. 193.4 million a year earlier, helped pre-tax profit rise 25 percent to Rs. 1.78 billion.\n\nQuarterly net interest income grew 11 percent to Rs. 3.61 billion. Interest income increased 36 percent to Rs. 10.41 billion, but interest expenses climbed faster, rising 55 percent to Rs. 6.81 billion.\n\nNet fee and commission income edged up 1 percent to Rs. 717.1 million. Total operating income increased 3 percent to Rs. 4.25 billion, while operating expenses rose 12 percent to Rs. 2.29 billion.\n\nFor the first nine months, net profit increased 19 percent to Rs. 3.59 billion, with earnings per share rising to Rs. 8.12.\n\nCumulative net interest income grew 12 percent to Rs. 10.68 billion, while net fee and commission income increased 17 percent to Rs. 2.18 billion. The bank attributed fee income growth to lending, trade finance, card usage and higher transaction volumes.\n\nHowever, its nine-month net interest margin narrowed to 4.16 percent from 4.55 percent. The cost-to-income ratio increased to 51.49 percent from 48.94 percent, while a reversal of excess tax provisions from previous years also supported cumulative earnings.\n\nThe bank said lower impairment charges reflected improved credit quality, recovery efforts and favourable changes in economic assumptions. Refinements to its expected credit loss models also affected impairment assessments."
  },
