    "md5": "90a8e379d52cc1452c6e7f3e7e58c1f0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "Health experts warn of disease outbreaks as floodwaters recede\n\nColombo, Nov. 2 (Daily Mirror) - Health experts have warned of a possible rise in waterborne and other infectious diseases over the next two weeks as floodwaters recede in several areas affected by minor flooding amid continued rainy weather.\n\nSpeaking to the Daily Mirror, Senior Consultant Physician Dr Ananda Wijewickrama said diseases such as leptospirosis, hepatitis A, typhoid and diarrhoeal illnesses could spread after flooding due to contaminated water sources.\n\nHe also warned that dengue remains a significant health threat during the rainy season, as the prevailing conditions could create favourable breeding grounds for mosquitoes.\n\nDr Wijewickrama said leptospirosis, commonly known as rat fever, was another major concern even following minor flooding.\n\n\u201cMinor flooded areas can become contaminated with the urine of infected animals, leading to the spread of leptospirosis,\u201d he said.\n\nHe added that contamination of drinking water sources during the monsoon season could also lead to an increase in diarrhoeal diseases.\n\nAlthough the effects may not be immediately apparent, he warned that such illnesses could emerge over the next two weeks as floodwaters recede.\n\nDr Wijewickrama stressed the importance of taking preventive measures, including ensuring access to safe drinking water, maintaining proper sanitation and practising good personal hygiene.\n\nHe also advised the public to avoid contact with muddy floodwater and consume only boiled or properly treated drinking water to minimise the risk of infection.\n\nAccording to the National Dengue Surveillance Unit, Sri Lanka had recorded 105,495 dengue cases as of midnight on October 27, with 81 deaths reported so far this year.\n\nGampaha District recorded the highest number of cases at 22,122, accounting for 20.97% of the total, followed by Colombo with 20,478 cases (19.41%) and Kandy with 8,440 cases (8.00%)."
