    "description": "Rs. 2.1tn unpaid and up to  Rs. 1tn in yearly BOI and other tax breaks test SL\u2019s 17% tax goal",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Rs-2-1tn-unpaid-and-up-to-Rs-1tn-in-yearly-BOI-and-other-tax-breaks-test-SLs-17-tax-goal/215-353969",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793604216.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-35830b16-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c61ee6249e7ed1629d10061bbb343155",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1570,
    "chunk_text": "Rs. 2.1tn unpaid and up to  Rs. 1tn in yearly BOI and other tax breaks test SL\u2019s 17% tax goal\n\nIMF names tax breaks incl. BOI regimes the primary source of revenue loss\n\nCompliant taxpayers bear the cost of weak enforcement, IMF warns\n\nTax breaks, including Board of Investment (BOI) incentives, are estimated to cost Sri Lanka Rs.800 billion to Rs.1 trillion a year, while tax defaults nearly doubled to Rs.2.1 trillion, exposing leaks that must be plugged to lift tax revenue to at least 17 percent of GDP by 2031, an International Monetary Fund (IMF) report showed.\n\nThe IMF identified policy-based tax exemptions and incentives, including BOI regimes, as the primary source of revenue loss in Sri Lanka. It put the foregone revenue at close to Rs.800 billion to Rs.1,000 billion annually in recent years, or roughly Rs.2.2 billion to Rs.2.7 billion a day.\n\nWithin the BOI regime, customs oversight of zone enterprises had rested with the BOI, which carried out no inspections and generally accepted documents as presented by operators.\n\n\u201cThe opportunities for revenue leakage are therefore immense,\u201d the report observed, noting that Sri Lanka Customs has made little progress in taking over those controls despite a 2025 agreement, as many companies resist closer scrutiny.\n\nThe MTRS objectives pledge to rationalise exemptions across special investment regimes and to limit new corporate income tax incentives to exceptional, narrowly defined cases.\n\nAnother section of the same document, however, refers to a commitment to introduce no new incentives at all."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-35830b16",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-35830b16",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Rs. 2.1tn unpaid and up to  Rs. 1tn in yearly BOI and other tax breaks test SL\u2019s 17% tax goal",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Rs-2-1tn-unpaid-and-up-to-Rs-1tn-in-yearly-BOI-and-other-tax-breaks-test-SLs-17-tax-goal/215-353969",
    "lang": "en",
