    "language": "en",
    "md5": "88bb7cc836ee3a42f812479a77ac36d2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "The MTRS objectives pledge to rationalise exemptions across special investment regimes and to limit new corporate income tax incentives to exceptional, narrowly defined cases.\n\nAnother section of the same document, however, refers to a commitment to introduce no new incentives at all.\n\nDebts older than five years \u201cappear to receive little or no collection action, even where they are not formally in dispute,\u201d the report noted. It added that the IRD cannot write off bad debts without referring them to the Finance Ministry, and that the arrears stock \u201cmay be overstated or poorly classified.\u201d\n\nThe IMF further observed that penalties, although available in law, are not applied consistently to late filing, non-filing or non-payment. That lapse undermines fairness, the report said, \u201cas compliant taxpayers bear the cost of meeting their obligations while persistent non-compliers may avoid timely consequences.\u201d\n\nCompliance gaps extend to VAT, where the IMF estimated that around 41 percent of potential revenue was lost to non-compliance in 2025, equivalent to about 3.6 percent of GDP. The VAT lost was therefore equal to roughly two-thirds of the 5.3 percent of GDP actually collected. Income tax filing was weaker still, with 52.7 percent of corporate returns and 67.8 percent of individual returns not filed at all in 2025, while international survey data for 2024 placed Sri Lanka last among selected South Asian peers on on-time filing.\n\nThe Finance Ministry published the diagnostic on October 30 alongside the objectives of the Medium-Term Revenue Strategy (MTRS) 2027-2031, fulfilling a structural benchmark under the Extended Fund Facility (EFF). The strategy targets tax revenue of at least 17 percent of GDP by 2031, when compared to a baseline of about 14.2 percent projected over 2027-2031 under the medium-term fiscal framework, implying roughly 2.8 percentage points of GDP in additional revenue."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
