    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "603302511813a673917854a953413fe2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "The Finance Ministry published the diagnostic on October 30 alongside the objectives of the Medium-Term Revenue Strategy (MTRS) 2027-2031, fulfilling a structural benchmark under the Extended Fund Facility (EFF). The strategy targets tax revenue of at least 17 percent of GDP by 2031, when compared to a baseline of about 14.2 percent projected over 2027-2031 under the medium-term fiscal framework, implying roughly 2.8 percentage points of GDP in additional revenue.\n\nThe VAT compliance gap alone exceeds that 2.8 percentage point shortfall. Yet the IMF\u2019s own estimate of what stronger inland revenue administration could deliver, about 1.9 percentage points of GDP after five years, would not close the gap on its own. The Fund stressed that the figure is illustrative, excludes Customs, Excise and tax policy measures, and should not be treated as revenue additional to the baseline.\n\nEven so, the strategy leans on the revenue agencies for its early gains. \u201cNear-term gains are expected mainly from stronger revenue administration and compliance,\u201d the MTRS stated.\n\nThe IMF, for its part, cautioned that \u201cwith many of the easier tax policy gains already realized, the MTRS should not rely mainly on further statutory rate increases.\u201d\n\nMuch of the post-crisis recovery has come from consumption taxes, according to the IMF. VAT rose to 5.3 percent of GDP in 2025 from 1.8 percent in 2021 and accounted for about half of the increase in tax revenue. Income tax rose to 3.5 percent of GDP from 1.9 percent over the same period, leaving VAT collections roughly one and a half times those of income tax.\n\nThe MTRS commits to \u201cprogressive tax policies\u201d that protect vulnerable households. It pledges to assess how VAT affects households across income levels, retain VAT exemptions only where they effectively protect poor and vulnerable households, and review how personal income tax can more effectively reach high-income individuals."
  },
