    "md5": "6da166475288b9cc56df02076bc2385b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1198,
    "chunk_text": "The MTRS commits to \u201cprogressive tax policies\u201d that protect vulnerable households. It pledges to assess how VAT affects households across income levels, retain VAT exemptions only where they effectively protect poor and vulnerable households, and review how personal income tax can more effectively reach high-income individuals.\n\nAdding to the vulnerabilities, the vendor maintenance contract for the IRD\u2019s core Revenue Administration Management Information System (RAMIS) expires on January 31, 2027. The IMF found that the planned transition to a local support model had not begun, and Excise\u2019s security sticker contract lapses the same month.\n\nIMF Division Chief Andrew Okello, who led the Fund\u2019s Fiscal Affairs Department mission to Colombo from June 24 to July 7, described the publication as another important step in advancing Sri Lanka\u2019s medium-term revenue reform agenda. \u201cPublication is an important milestone, but ultimately implementation and measurable results will determine the success of the strategy,\u201d he wrote in a LinkedIn post.\n\nThe report itself warned that \u201cthe principal implementation risk is a loss of momentum once the immediate crisis context fades.\u201d"
