    "description": "Independent probes on police brutality a must!",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Independent-probes-on-police-brutality-a-must/172-353971",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793604856.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-5984a69c-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c16fbd3a5fcf8d784670f0771e2a4696",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1379,
    "chunk_text": "Independent probes on police brutality a must!\n\nTwo cases of police brutality, one in Homagama and another in Kobeigane where two suspects taken into custody died under mysterious circumstances have raised renewed concerns regarding the conduct of the Police.\n\nOfficers at the Homagama police station or the Police media unit have failed to provide a clear response to this particular incident. With regards to the Kobeigane incident,\u00a0 three officers attached to the Kobeigane police station were initially suspended and interdicted over dereliction of duty and failure to provide medical care.\n\nOn October 31, police opened fire at a fleeing three-wheeler in Mahakade junction, Kadawatha. A 16-year old boy traveling in the three-wheeler sustained injuries. The driver, reportedly a 17-year old individual fled the scene as he had no valid driving license. He had later surrendered to the police. Investigations are underway with regards to these three incidents.\n\nIf there are lapses pertaining to custodial deaths, it sets a bad precedent regarding the duties of the police. The incumbent government has a duty to acknowledge these incidents and ensure that justice is being done to perpetrators. The careless way in which the police responds to some of these incidents doesn\u2019t look good on the apex institution that is responsible to maintain law and order in the country."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Democracy: Bridge to power or commitment to values?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Democracy-Bridge-to-power-or-commitment-to-values/172-353972",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793604942.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "12b52c6dd832f6061b5a9d089886f9a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1543,
    "chunk_text": "Democracy: Bridge to power or commitment to values?\n\nIn politics, democracy is touted as the best form of governance. Regular elections give people the opportunity to choose their rulers and remove them when they fail to meet public expectations.\n\nBe that as it may, democracy is riddled with weaknesses. Political parties often champion democratic rights when they are relegated to the opposition, only to become less enthusiastic about those principles once they assume office. The temptation to consolidate power at the expense of democratic values is a recurring feature of politics. Sri Lanka is no exception.\n\nThe ancient Greek philosopher, Plato warned that excessive freedom, coupled with a lack of discipline, could lead to disorder and ultimately create conditions in which a tyrant rises to power. In modern democracies, the danger is not confined to the rise of an individual tyrant. The electoral process itself can be manipulated through misinformation, populist rhetoric and the exploitation of public grievances. In today\u2019s social media era, false or misleading information spreads rapidlyWhen political decisions are driven by misinformation rather than informed debate, democracy risks becoming a contest in which perception matters more than substance.\n\nManipulation of public opinion can also make it difficult for governments to take unpopular but necessary decisions in the national interest. Yet the greater danger arises when those who win elections begin to regard their mandate as an unrestricted licence to govern."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Democracy: Bridge to power or commitment to values?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Democracy-Bridge-to-power-or-commitment-to-values/172-353972",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793604942.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d7258f60e96cdfdafc212f8658d6ec25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "Manipulation of public opinion can also make it difficult for governments to take unpopular but necessary decisions in the national interest. Yet the greater danger arises when those who win elections begin to regard their mandate as an unrestricted licence to govern.\n\nHowever, the Yahapalana government made only marginal progress in fulfilling its promises of democratic transformation. The\u00a0 draconian Prevention of Terrorism Act (PTA)\u00a0 \u00a0was expected to be repealed. The task remained unfinished, while the Act\u2019s provisions were also invoked against political opponents.\n\nThe Gotabaya Rajapaksa administration, elected in 2019, subsequently faced the COVID-19 pandemic and a devastating economic crisis. The Aragalaya protests eventually forced the President to leave office. The upheaval showed that electoral victories cannot indefinitely shield governments from public discontent. It also underlined the need for political leaders to remain accountable between elections, rather than relying solely on the mandate secured at the ballot box.\n\nThe National People\u2019s Power (NPP), which championed democratic rights while in opposition, was at the forefront of demonstrations against the PTA. Now in office, however, its continued resort to the Act against individuals regarded as political opponents raises questions about the consistency of its commitment to reform. The prolonged detention of former Eastern Province Chief Minister Sivanesathurai Chandrakanthan is a case in point. The circumstances and legal grounds of individual detentions must be examined on their merits, but the broader question remains: can a law condemned by a party in opposition become acceptable merely because that party now exercises executive power?\n\nWorse, it has introduced the Prevention of State from Terrorism Bill to be enacted in place of the PTA. It sounds even more draconian."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-72f6662f",
    "date_str": "2026-11-02",
    "description": "Democracy: Bridge to power or commitment to values?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Democracy-Bridge-to-power-or-commitment-to-values/172-353972",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793604942.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-02-dailymirrorlk-72f6662f-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "5fcb891155a55d4eab92793c5ab4b339",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1348,
    "chunk_text": "The National People\u2019s Power (NPP), which championed democratic rights while in opposition, was at the forefront of demonstrations against the PTA. Now in office, however, its continued resort to the Act against individuals regarded as political opponents raises questions about the consistency of its commitment to reform. The prolonged detention of former Eastern Province Chief Minister Sivanesathurai Chandrakanthan is a case in point. The circumstances and legal grounds of individual detentions must be examined on their merits, but the broader question remains: can a law condemned by a party in opposition become acceptable merely because that party now exercises executive power?\n\nWorse, it has introduced the Prevention of State from Terrorism Bill to be enacted in place of the PTA. It sounds even more draconian.\n\nBritish writer George Orwell explored the relationship between political power, misinformation and the distortion of truth. In his essay *Politics and the English Language*, he observed that political language could be used to make falsehoods appear truthful.\n\nElections provide the route to power, but democratic principles determine how that power should be exercised. The real test of a government is not how loudly it champions civil liberties while in opposition, but how committed\u00a0 it is to protect\u00a0 those rights."
  },
  {
