    "chunk_size_bytes": 1815,
    "chunk_text": "Caste Oppression and Struggles in Jaffna\n\nI recently visited one of the primary schools in an oppressed caste village in Karainagar. The coastal village on the lagoon side of the Karainagar Island in northern Jaffna was hard to reach with the road in a disintegrating state.\n\nThe little over one hundred students in the school live within walking distance. As I began the discussion with the principal about the childrens\u2019 education and health, it became apparent that the principal had a clear understanding of not just the needs of the school and the students, but also the socio-economic challenges facing the community embedding the school. The social exclusion of this community from transport access to the village, to the lack of livelihood opportunities for the families, and the bleak future for its youth troubled him. I had been going to this village from time to time over the last twelve years. It started with an invitation by a priest who wanted some of us to come see a church-run after school programme in the early 2010s, which was initiated with resettlement after the war\u2019s end. The underlying caste dynamics were already apparent. Bus service for children to reach the secondary school was disrupted, post-war housing schemes were wanting and the local bureaucracy was not forthcoming with the public services necessary in the village. Poverty was visibly high, and older children were seeking wage work. I asked the principal about that after school programme, but he said it was no longer functioning, and that it fell apart after the priest was transferred.\n\nMinister of Finance Dr. N. M. Perera\u00a0 created the Palm Development Co-operative Societies (PDCS) in 1972, and provided them the monopoly of production and sale of toddy (images courtesy of HERB EXCELLENCE and Mauritius Images)."
  },
