    "chunk_size_bytes": 1619,
    "chunk_text": "Minister of Finance Dr. N. M. Perera\u00a0 created the Palm Development Co-operative Societies (PDCS) in 1972, and provided them the monopoly of production and sale of toddy (images courtesy of HERB EXCELLENCE and Mauritius Images).\n\nSixty years ago in October 1966, a tremendous movement in Jaffna defeated untouchability. That powerful anti-caste struggle and its legacy of mobilisation, and the eventual emergence of co-operatives for the oppressed caste toddy tappers, have many lessons for the role of youth and social change. Devastated by close to three decades of armed conflict, and a hopeless recovery spanning over a decade and a half, what are the possibilities for social transformation in Jaffna today? I return here to the conjuncture in the 1960s to think about how economic and political changes brought a major shift in caste relations, and consider strategies to address caste oppression and exclusion in Jaffna society today.\n\nHistorically, Jaffna had unique social and economic characteristics with highly populated small holding agriculture, where the dominant Vellala caste was also the numerical majority. This provided little room for resistance by the oppressed castes. Rural accumulation with import substitution policies starting in the late 1950s led to further intensified agriculture for cash crops on small plots in Jaffna; many small farmers claim it is from such accumulation that they could build cement houses. In this context, with increased demand for wage labour from the landless oppressed castes, who also began demanding higher wages, the upper castes turned to further repression."
  },
