    "chunk_text": "Historically, Jaffna had unique social and economic characteristics with highly populated small holding agriculture, where the dominant Vellala caste was also the numerical majority. This provided little room for resistance by the oppressed castes. Rural accumulation with import substitution policies starting in the late 1950s led to further intensified agriculture for cash crops on small plots in Jaffna; many small farmers claim it is from such accumulation that they could build cement houses. In this context, with increased demand for wage labour from the landless oppressed castes, who also began demanding higher wages, the upper castes turned to further repression.\n\nThe struggle launched in October 1966 was perhaps the first armed struggle in Sri Lanka\u2019s postcolonial history; as many as thirty people died in pitched battles and murders. While the two armed insurrections in the South in 1971 and 1988, as well as the armed insurgency turned civil war in the North, all of which led to horrendous loss of life, the anti-caste struggle in the late 1960s came to an end with the concrete outcome of more or less eradicating untouchability.\n\nIn this context, the leftist Minister of Finance Dr. N. M. Perera of the United Front Government created the Palm Development Co-operative Societies (PDCS) in 1972, and provided them the monopoly of production and sale of toddy. Forty years later, when I interviewed some individuals who as youth were part of the anti-caste movement, I learned that many of them after the struggle had become employees of the newly formed PDCS. However, that momentum to address caste oppression were over-determined by ethno-nationalist politics and the protracted conflict, with the anti-caste work receding to the background."
