    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "In this context, the leftist Minister of Finance Dr. N. M. Perera of the United Front Government created the Palm Development Co-operative Societies (PDCS) in 1972, and provided them the monopoly of production and sale of toddy. Forty years later, when I interviewed some individuals who as youth were part of the anti-caste movement, I learned that many of them after the struggle had become employees of the newly formed PDCS. However, that momentum to address caste oppression were over-determined by ethno-nationalist politics and the protracted conflict, with the anti-caste work receding to the background.\n\nThe principal of the school above told me his most recent challenge is that many mothers of his students have just started working long hours in a new seafood processing plant in the village, which means the children get even less attention at home with\u00a0 higher absenteeism of students. While the families needed additional income, they also required additional support for the welfare of their children.\n\nFor the oppressed caste communities, their troubles are inter-twined from lack of land and access to coastal resources resulting in dwindling livelihoods, exploitative working conditions when they go for wage labour, inability to access public services and often outright discrimination. They are even subject to abuse by dominant caste actors including job providers and officials.\n\nIn the meantime, Jaffna is also going through social and political changes. A nihilist culture including incidents of gang violence and misogynist attacks against women are rising; even though women are socially advancing with a larger share in higher education and white collar jobs. For most young men and women, job opportunities are few, and the availability of and the remuneration for wage labour are low."
  },
