    "chunk_size_bytes": 1672,
    "chunk_text": "Now it looks as if the two, Nazrul Islam and Siti Akhtar, have been working in Saudi Arabia for some time. Their ages and marital status has not been confirmed. What is confirmed is that both were executed on Oct. 14 under Islamic law for committing an unspecified \u2018immoral act\u2019 inside a mosque. The general consensus is that it was a kiss.\n\nBangladesh has demanded an explanation. But the country has over three million workers in Saudi Arabia, and is not in a position to take any strong action.\n\nIn most countries, adultery is a case for civil courts and does not carry a death sentence (except for several Islamic countries). In the US, however, A few states (such as Wisconsin, Michigan, Minnesota, and Idaho) still have active laws\u00a0 that classify adultery as a misdemeanor or felony, though actual criminal prosecution is extremely rare.\n\nKissing is not a legal offense around the world, including Sri Lanka. But police here use antiquated vagrancy laws to prosecute couples for \u2018immoral behaviour\u2019 in public places. Except for several Islamic countries such as Saudi Arabia, conditions wary widely \u2013 In China, according to custom, public displays of affection (PDAs) are not the norm. At the University of Zimbabwe, students can be expelled for PDA. In Indonesia, usually a liberal country, kissing or even handholding between couples in public is not accepted.\n\nThailand is famous for its sex industry, but it is a conservative country and kissing in public is not allowed. In the Middle East, PDAs including kissing in public between opposite genders is now allowed, though family members and\u00a0 friends can kiss on the cheeks as a gesture of affection."
  },
