    "chunk_size_bytes": 1354,
    "chunk_text": "Due to a lack of transparency, the numbers reported by ESOHR are minimum counts. ESOHR noted that Saudi Arabia\u2019s continued expanded use of the death penalty contradicts previous promises made by Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman\u00a0 to limit its use in the Kingdom.\n\nTheir families said\u00a0 confessions were given under torture, and the accused often could not afford legal representation. Previously, prisoners would receive notice of their imminent execution and be afforded the opportunity to call their families, but recently, some families have reported only being notified of a loved one\u2019s execution after the fact. This is precisely what happened in the case of the two executed Bangladeshis. Even the government was not given prior notice.\n\nWith Dubai losing its lustre as a top Mideastern tourist destination, Saudi Arabia is investing heavily to lure millions of tourists to the Red Sea. The Red Sea Destination is a massive, luxury regenerative tourism megaproject along the country\u2019s west coast.\n\nBut is Saudi Arabia ready to apply the same unforgiving laws to those wealthy tourists who would be mainly from the West? Or is it going to apply a different standard? Executing two poor Bangladeshis from a nation struggling to develop is one thing \u2013 doing that for millionaire types throwing around money into Saudi coffers would be another."
